{{! /freestyle/start — novice entry page. A first session for a visitor with no
    freestyle background: what the sport is, what to try physically, and where
    to go after the basics. Plain language only: terms (stall, set, catch,
    dexterity) are defined here before they are used, and no notation
    abbreviations appear on this page. }}

<div class="hero hero-sm">
  <div>
    {{#if navigation.breadcrumbs}}
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      {{#each navigation.breadcrumbs}}
        {{#if href}}<a href="{{href}}">{{label}}</a> <span class="breadcrumb-sep">/</span> {{else}}<span>{{label}}</span>{{/if}}
      {{/each}}
    </nav>
    {{/if}}
    <h1>{{page.title}}</h1>
    {{#if page.intro}}<p class="hero-subtitle">{{page.intro}}</p>{{/if}}
  </div>
</div>

<div class="wrapper longform-article">

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 class="section-heading">What you need</h2>
    <p>
      A footbag (any small, soft, slightly under-filled bag works to start),
      flat-soled shoes with a usable toe and inside surface, and a small clear
      space. That is all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Contact and control</h2>
    <p>
      Your first goal is to touch the bag and make it go where you want. Drop
      it onto the top of your foot near the toes, the <strong>toe
      surface</strong>, and pop it straight up into your hands. Then do the
      same with the <strong>inside surface</strong>, the flat inner edge of
      the shoe. Five catches in a row from each surface, and you have the two
      contacts most of freestyle is built on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 class="section-heading">First moves</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>stall</strong> is catching the bag flat on a surface so it
      rests instead of bouncing away. Start with the toe stall: meet the
      falling bag with the top of your foot and lower the foot as it lands,
      like catching an egg.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>set</strong> sends the bag up; a <strong>catch</strong>
      regains control, usually as a stall. Tricks happen between the two.
      From a toe stall, pop the bag to head height and stall it again.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>dexterity</strong> (players say "dex") is a leg circling
      around the bag while it is in the air, without touching it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Your first trick: the Mirage</h2>
    <p>
      The Mirage is among the first complete dexterity tricks many players
      learn. Set the bag straight up from one toe. As it falls, circle it from
      the inside to the outside with the opposite leg, then
      catch the bag back on the same toe that made the set. The
      circling leg performs the dexterity but does not make the catch.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/media/browse?context=mirage" class="action-link">Watch a Mirage Clip</a>
      {{#if content.mirageFamilyHref}}<a href="{{siteHref content.mirageFamilyHref}}" class="action-link">Read About the Mirage</a>{{/if}}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="next-steps">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Next steps</h2>
    <p>
      Practice stalls on both feet and the Mirage in both directions.
      <a href="/media/freestyle-tutorials">The tutorial collections</a> hold
      lesson videos for dozens more tricks, and
      <a href="/freestyle/about">About Freestyle</a> covers competition formats
      and judging. {{content.nextStepsIntro}}
    </p>
    <ol>
      {{#each content.nextSteps}}
      <li>{{#if href}}<a href="{{siteHref href}}">{{name}}</a>{{else}}{{name}}{{/if}}: {{lesson}}</li>
      {{/each}}
    </ol>
  </section>

  {{> back-link}}
</div>
